uery is likely due to the inefficient planning and execution plan generated by the database. The query involves multiple joins, indexes, and filters, which can lead to a complex execution plan that takes a long time to execute.\n\nThe use of CTEs (Common Table Expressions) can help improve performance by allowing the planner to materialize the intermediate results, reducing the complexity of the execution plan.\n\n**Case 2: Different query results**\n\nThis is an interesting case!\n\nThe difference in results between the two queries lies in how `NOT IN` and `MINUS` operators behave when dealing with duplicate rows or NULL values.\n\nIn the first query, `MINUS` eliminates all rows that match the subquery, including duplicates. In contrast, `NOT IN` only eliminates distinct rows that match the subquery.\n\nIf you have a table with duplicate rows or NULL values in the `status_code` column, this difference can lead to different results between the two queries.\n\nTo illustrate this, consider the following example:\n\n| field_1 | field_2 | status_code |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A | B | 3 |\n| A | B | 10 |\n\nThe first query (`MINUS`) would eliminate both rows, while the second query (`NOT IN`) would only eliminate one row.\n\nIn summary, the slow query can be improved by using CTEs or rewriting the query to reduce complexity. The different query results are due to the behavior of `NOT IN` and `MINUS` operators when dealing with duplicate rows or NULL values.', 'time': 3.1107730865478516}
